# interplay_versions_live_30_to_245: The version changed while tasks live (245 to 30 and back, and 30 to 245 and back). Task 1, defined at the first version, lists each gated object (two-plane textures, one and two levels; a shared texture; a tracked heap; a read-only buffer and heap) at that version, then again after the change (made objects are cached by reference; a refused one is tried again), then a second set of the same kinds under new references; task 2 is defined after the change and lists its own. The device's gates follow the change at once (67 at the new version); in-process tasks create objects by the device's current features, the service's by the copy the task was created with, so the setups differ exactly where task 1's new objects meet a flag the two versions disagree on. The guest lays out each two-plane texture's stencil plane with its current version's texel (4 bytes below 62, 1 from it: s8ByteCountForD32S8), so a texture the 30 guest writes is refused by a task that reads 245's features (task 1 in the service; task 2 in-process once back at 245) though both allow two-plane textures. Back at the first version, task 2's objects are listed again.
